and I think you can be concerned that  there's not enough mobility or enough  opportunity but you don't have to also  believe that things are getting worse hi  I'm Nicolas be with Reason TV and today  we're talking with Scott Winship he is a  PhD fellow at the Brookings Institution  in Washington DC Scott thanks for  talking to us oh my pleasure things so  you do a lot of work on kind of related  concepts of income inequality and income  mobility everybody seems to be  interested in any income inequality the  gap between the wealthiest and the  poorest people also talk a lot about  income ability the ability of a person  to move from kind of one end of income  spectrum to the other and everybody  whatever their you know whatever their  ideology is seems to agree income  inequality has spread more and that  income mobility is over you have people  like charles murray and coming apart  thing it's over timothy noah in his  recent book about income mobility saying  it's over  you take issue with that statement talk  a little bit about what your work on  income ability is found peoples kind of  talk researchers journalists talk  offhandedly about the American Dream  declining the mobility has declined the  research is much more ambiguous than  that it's not clear at all that mobility  has declined and in part I think people  are wedded to this idea that it has  declined because there is a consensus as  you say that inequality has increased  and so people talk about the rungs of  the income ladder growing further apart  and it's so I mean they're basically  saying okay because the richest 1% or  the richest 10% is much richer than the  lowest 10% then mobility between these  sections have to become it has to be  harder that's right  where inequality is really taking off is  at the very top of the ladder and so if  you're using this ladder metaphor you  know what's happened is the top rung has  has flown away from everybody else but  there's no reason that that makes it any  more difficult to climb you know the  bottom two-thirds of the ladder for  instance so from your research basically  what you're finding is that it's it's  not that everybody has  an equal shot at becoming a  bazillionaire or anything but that the  rates of mobility in 1970 are roughly  the same as they are now that's right  and I think you can be concerned that  there's not enough mobility or enough  opportunity but you don't have to also  believe that things are getting worse  right but and yet when you've got it to  print in a variety of places you've  talked about this at everywhere from  national review to the Atlantic to your  own work at Brookings and whatnot you  routinely get attacked for you know why  are people so mad if you're actually  bringing some good news I think there's  an analogy on the right where if you're  a cultural conservative for instance  that you know the gay uncle and your  family in some sense is this existential  you know threat to your to your  worldview and it becomes I think you  have a similar situation on the left or  if you start to acknowledge that some  things actually are not getting worse or  getting better it it starts to chip away  at the whole bundle of commitments is  there an easy way to say let's say you  you were born right at the poverty line  what are what are your odds of becoming  fully middle class or upper middle class  or rich so if you're born in the bottom  fifth you have about a 40 percent chance  that you're actually going to stay there  when you're an adult as well now people  look at that and think oh that's that's  great you know 60 percent make it out  and other people look at it and say well  this is a travesty the way that I tend  to put it in context is if you start in  the bottom what are the chances that you  end up in the top to fifth so kind of  the solid middle class upper middle  class it turns out you have about a 17  percent chance of doing this but how do  we jack up the 17% chance that a  low-income person has to get up to the  top forty percent of income there is a  small revolution I think that's starting  to happen with randomized controlled  trials and and really trying to figure  out what works and to try to pursue  those policies and to abandon ones that  don't including some relatively popular  ones things like head start that  actually don't look like they they have  much longer term impact and that's an  early intervention preschool program and  early early elementary school program  that's right the primary way that the  federal government  nests in import kids before school how  did you get interested in questions of  income ability since being in college  I've been in urban places and have  noticed the inequality and have had  spent a lot of time really thinking  about the disadvantages of growing up in  concentrated poverty in poor  neighborhoods and and and that's really  what originally got me interested in it  as well and and then as I found out over  time you know a lot of the economic  trends that I think liberals tend to  overstate how how bad things are getting  to the extent that some of those  actually don't look all that bad at all  I think what is left of the problems  that are kind of worth worrying about to  me a limited opportunity people who  start in the bottom kind of rises to the  top by default I guess well Scott  Winship of the Brookings Institution  thank you so much for talking to Reason  TV today about income mobility I'm Nick  Gillespie for Reason TV
 

